\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We present a novel multi-stage method for colour image segmentation, with a primary focus on vessels segmentation in retinal fundus images, using a U-Net based architecture. Our approach tackles challenges posed by varying image resolutions through a coarse-to-fine segmentation pipeline. It begins with a rough segmentation at varying scales, guided by a traditional CNN and progressively refines results to find a target resolution. It culminates with detailed segmentation at a target scale with a smaller window sliding step, compared to previous stages. We train and validate our method using four publicly available datasets FIVES, DRHAGIS, HRF, and STARE - and demonstrate superior performance compared to traditional sliding window techniques. Notably, our model achieves high accuracy with relatively few training images. The entire framework is open-sourced and adaptable to a wide range of image segmentation tasks.
\end{abstract}

\keywords{U-Net, segmentation, deep learning, vessel segmentation, medical imaging, fundus images}

\section{Introduction}

The process of image segmentation has been for many years a challenge, that was only partially solveable. This changed with an advent of deep learning and introduction of convoutional neural networks. In the year 2012 Krizhevsky et al \cite{krizhevsky2012imagenet} introduced ImageNet, which achieved a new level of image recognition accuracy. It won the ImageNet competition that year, and inspired a plethora of other researchers to improve upon it. This milestone in image classification allowed a development of new class of segmentation algorithms.

One of the challenges in research was to segment biomedical images. It was partially solved by Ronneberger et al. \cite{ronneberger2015unet} by introduction of \textit{U-Net} architecture. The \textit{U-Net} is designed to work with very few training images and to yield more precise segmentations. It consists of a contracting path to capture context and a symmetric expanding path that enables precise localization. However it comes with drawbacks, such as sensitivity to input image resolution and segmentation window position, and high demand for GPU memory which is limiting the segmentation window size (\textit{WS}).

In this article, we address challenges in image segmentation by proposing a multi-step framework for training a \textit{U-Net} adaptable to varying image resolutions. We evaluate segmentation results at multiple scales to select the most effective output and analyze how resolution affects performance when the model is applied at different scales. We also assess the annotation effort required to achieve satisfactory results. Experiments on four public vessel segmentation datasets enable us to test how well a \textit{U-Net} trained on one dataset generalizes to others.

The article has six chapters. The first introduces the topic. The second reviews recent work on \textit{U-Net} segmentation in medical imaging. The third explains our approach with illustrations. The fourth describes our experiments and results. The fifth highlights key findings. The final chapter states there are no competing interests.

The main contributions of this work are:
\begin{itemize} 
  \item A multi-scale rough segmentation step that processes images at multiple predefined scales using a sliding window approach. 
  \item A \textit{CNN}-based scoring mechanism that selects the optimal segmentation scale for each image based on predicted segmentation quality. 
  \item Experimental validation of cross-dataset generalization, demonstrating that models trained on one dataset can perform well on others. 
  \item An analysis of how the number of training images affects segmentation quality, providing insight into the annotation effort required for satisfactory performance. 
  \item An open-source implementation of the method, available on GitHub \cite{palles77unetsegmentation}.
\end{itemize}

\section{Related works}

Ren et al. \cite{ren2022improved} proposed an improved \textit{U-Net}-based method for retinal vessel image segmentation, enhancing the original architecture to achieve greater accuracy and robustness. Their improvements include modifications to both the network structure and the training process. Liu et al. \cite{liu2023retina} introduced a three-path \textit{U-Net} model for retina image segmentation, which leverages multiple pathways to capture features at different levels, resulting in improved segmentation performance. Das et al. \cite{das2024assessment} evaluated the performance of the standard \textit{U-Net} for retinal blood vessel segmentation, confirming its effectiveness for this task. Yun et al. \cite{yun2020multi} proposed a Multi-Path Recurrent \textit{U-Net} for segmenting retinal fundus images, incorporating both multiple paths and recurrent units to enhance feature extraction. Similarly, Huang et al. \cite{huang2023retinal} developed an improved \textit{U-Net} architecture based on residual modules, achieving increased robustness and better segmentation results in retinal vascular images.

Many existing studies aim to enhance the performance of \textit{U-Net} across a range of applications. A common theme among these efforts is the focus on segmenting disconnected or localized objects \cite{Westwanska2019}, often overlooking use cases like vessel segmentation, where structures are continuous and span the entire image. Additionally, relatively few works address the challenge of handling images with varying resolutions - a critical factor in real-world medical imaging scenarios. Notably, many proposed improvements to \textit{U-Net} result in only marginal performance gains, typically in the range of 1–2\% absolute accuracy, as the original \textit{U-Net} already performs strongly, often achieving over 95\% accuracy under favorable conditions. 

\section{Methodology}

In recent years, numerous high-quality datasets for fndus eye images (\textit{FEI}) were published. We selected four publicly available datasets for our experiments: \textit{FIVES} \cite{jin2022fives}, consisting of 1,200 images with a resolution of 2048$\times$2048 pixels; \textit{DRHAGIS} \cite{holm2017drhagis}, containing 80 images at 4752$\times$3168 pixels; \textit{HRF} \cite{budai2013robust}, which includes 45 images at 3504$\times$2336 pixels; and \textit{STARE} \cite{hoover2000locating}, from which we used 20 images at 700$\times$605 pixels resolution.

\subsection{Training data preparation}
\label{subsec:step2}

In preparing data for \textit{U-Net} training, it is essential to extract square windows (ideally with dimensions of $2^n$) from the input images. These windows are then fed into the training pipeline to iteratively refine the model's performance. Although \textit{U-Net} is known for its efficiency and accuracy under favorable conditions, a significant challenge lies in determining which parts of the image should be selected for training.To address this challenge, several key questions must be considered:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Which locations should be chosen to ensure that windows cover both object and background regions effectively?
  \item How many windows should be sampled from each image?
  \item How many of these windows should predominantly contain the object of interest, and how many background?
\end{itemize}

Below, we present our approach to answering the forementioned questions by constructing a mechanism for automatic data generation. We use the \textit{FIVES} dataset \cite{jin2022fives} as the primary input for training the \textit{U-Net} model. The original dataset, consisting of 1,200 images, is divided into two subsets. The first subset, referred to as the \textit{U-Net} Main Training Set (\textit{UNETMTS}), includes 900 images and is used for model training. The second subset, called the \textit{U-Net} Segmentation Validation Set (\textit{UNETSVS}), comprises the remaining 300 images and is used to validate segmentation performance.

\subsubsection{Mask finding}
\label{subsubsection:maskfinding}

Each image is processed as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Convert from RGB to grayscale.
  \item Compute a threshold:
  \[
  \text{threshold} = \frac{\text{mean}(\text{grayscale\_image})}{3} - 5
  \]
  and binarize to generate a mask.
  \item Apply flood fill from all four corners to produce an additional mask.
  \item Combine the two masks by pixel-wise summation.
  \item Apply erosion and dilation with a $20 \times 20$ kernel.
\end{enumerate}

Finally, the mask is applied via logical AND operation. Pixels within its outer layer are blacked out; others remain unchanged (see Fig.~\ref{fig:image_masking}).

\begin{figure}[ht]
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{@{}l@{\hspace{5px}}l@{\hspace{5px}}l@{}}
    \includegraphics[width=0.3\linewidth]{figures/input_train.png} &
    \includegraphics[width=0.3\linewidth]{figures/input_train_mask.png} &
    \includegraphics[width=0.3\linewidth]{figures/input_train_masked.png} \\
    \scriptsize (a) Input image & \scriptsize (b) Mask & \scriptsize (c) Masked image \\
  \end{tabular}
  \caption{Image Masking Results}
  \label{fig:image_masking}
\end{figure}

After the mask is generated, the image is trimmed from the left, right, top, and bottom edges to ensure that no completely black vertical or horizontal lines remain.

\subsubsection{Random windows}
\label{subsubsec:randomwindows}

The initial set, \textit{UNETMTS}, is reduced to a specified value referred to as \textit{TRDTSZ} (training data size), which is initially set to 900. However, this value is varied and analyzed in later sections of this article (see Section~\ref{sec:experiments}) to investigate whether the full set of 900 images is necessary, or if smaller subsets can still yield high segmentation accuracy. The goal is to investigate how much training data the \textit{U-Net} model requires to achieve optimal results. Once the value of \textit{TRDTSZ} is selected, \textit{UNETMTS} is randomly divided into two subsets: a training set (\textit{DPSTR}) and a test set (\textit{DPSTS}), using a 75\% to 25\% split.

From each image, a set of training windows is randomly sampled. Two types of windows are defined: object-type windows (\textit{OBTW}) and background-type windows (\textit{BKTW}). A window is classified as \textit{OBTW} if the number of pixels corresponding to the ground truth label exceeds a minimum threshold (e.g., 15\%). Conversely, a window is labeled as \textit{BKTW} if the proportion of object pixels does not exceed a maximum threshold (e.g., 5\%). We also define target ratio of object to background windows per image (e.g., 50\%), to ensure a representative sampling that reflects typical pixel distribution between object and background regions in \textit{FEI}.

The underlying motivation for this windowing strategy is to balance object and background representation during training. This balance is reinforced by employing a weighted binary cross-entropy loss function in the \textit{U-Net} implementation, which helps to address class imbalance. The size of each sampled window (\textit{WS}) is typically set to either 64$\times$64 or 128$\times$128 pixels. Input images are rescaled after mask generation to 512$\times$512 pixels. Larger values of \textit{WS} and image resolution significantly increase GPU memory consumption - an important consideration given that our hardware setup was a GPU with only 8GB of memory.

The percentage values used in this step are based on intuition and can be adjusted depending on the type of images, particularly when the ratio of object pixels to background pixels varies. For instance, these values would differ when segmenting structures such as the eye cup (\textit{EC}) or optic disc (\textit{ED}) in other \textit{FEI} datasets. The goal of this strategy is to capture a wide range of spatial arrangements of the target object within randomly selected windows, thereby enriching the training dataset for the \textit{U-Net} model used in later stages of the system.

\subsection{U-Net network training}
\label{subsec:step3)}

The windows extracted in Section~\ref{subsec:step2} are grouped into training batches, with a predefined number of epochs and a selected (\textit{WS}) that matches the input dimensions of the \textit{U-Net} model. The input windows, referred to as \textit{X} windows, consist of RGB channels, with pixel values normalized to the range [0, 1]. The corresponding output windows \textit{Y} windows, represent the segmentation masks, and are converted into binary image.

\subsection{U-Net preliminary multi resolution segmentation}
\label{subsec:step4}

This step begins with a rough segmentation step (\textit{RSS}). Input image is scaled to a resolution of 512$\times$512, then segmented using a sliding window technique. Its primary goal is to produce preliminary segmentation results which are passed to the next stage Section~\ref{subsec:step5}, where a dedicated \textit{CNN} model is trained how to evaluate segmentation result.

\subsubsection{Windows mesh creation}
\label{subssubsec:windows_mesh_creation}

Once the image has been prepared for segmentation - by generating a mask as described in Section~\ref{subsubsection:maskfinding} - it typically contains a black border surrounding the scene, with the retina centered. This preprocessed image is then analyzed across three color channels: red, green, and blue. Each color channel is divided into a collection of meshes (i.e., image patches) by sliding a window across the image. Mesh extraction begins with an offset of 0 and proceeds up to $\textit{WS}-1$, using a predefined sliding step denoted as \textit{SS}. While it is not required for $\textit{WS} \bmod \textit{SS} = 0$, it is essential that $\textit{SS} < \textit{WS}$ to ensure overlapping patches and continuous coverage of the entire image. The idea of meshes is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:meshes}

\begin{figure}[ht]
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{@{}l@{\hspace{5px}}l@{\hspace{5px}}l@{}@{\hspace{5px}}l@{}@{\hspace{5px}}l@{}}
    \includegraphics[width=0.23\linewidth]{figures/mesh_offset_0.png} &
    \includegraphics[width=0.23\linewidth]{figures/mesh_offset_1.png} &
    \includegraphics[width=0.23\linewidth]{figures/mesh_offset_2.png} &
    \includegraphics[width=0.23\linewidth]{figures/mesh_offset_3.png} \\	
    \scriptsize (a) Offset 0 & \scriptsize (b) Offset 32 & \scriptsize (c) Offset 64  & \scriptsize (d) Offset 96 \\
  \end{tabular}
  \caption{Sample meshes generated for an image of size 1024$\times$1024 pixels, \textit{WS} of 128$\times$128 pixels, and \textit{WSS} set to 32}
  \label{fig:meshes}
\end{figure}

Each mesh allows the input image to be segmented using a different window configuration. This approach is crucial for capturing diverse spatial variations within the image, as each mesh is processed independently by the trained \textit{U-Net} model. For each mesh, the \textit{U-Net} generates a probability map, assigning to each pixel the likelihood of belonging to the object or background. Due to overlapping meshes, a single pixel may appear in multiple segmentations, resulting in multiple probability estimates. These values are averaged to generate a heatmap, which represents the overall likelihood of each pixel being part of the object. In this heatmap, brighter pixels correspond to higher confidence in object presence. Finally, the heatmap is binarized using a fixed threshold of 127, producing a black-and-white segmentation mask. An example of this process is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:meshes}, where four meshes contribute to the heatmap visualized in Fig.~\ref{fig:heatmap1}. This method is referred to as the Sliding Window Algorithm (\textit{SWA}).

\begin{figure}[ht]
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{@{}l@{\hspace{5px}}l@{\hspace{5px}}l@{}}
    \includegraphics[height=0.10\textheight]{figures/presegment_rough_original.png} &
    \includegraphics[height=0.10\textheight]{figures/presegment_rough_heatmap.png} \\
    \scriptsize (a) Input image & \scriptsize (b) Pre binarization heatmap \\
  \end{tabular}
  \caption{Input image from \cite{jin2022fives} along with generated heatmap}
  \label{fig:heatmap1}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Data preparation for \textit{CNN} training}

As described in Section~\ref{subsec:step2}, the main dataset \cite{jin2022fives} was partitioned to include a subset named \textit{UNETSVS}, which is used for training the \textit{CNN} in the subsequent evaluation stage. All images in this subset are processed through the (\textit{RSS}). We perform segmentation at multiple scales, ranging from 20\% to 100\% of the original image size, in increments of 10\%. This produces a series of black-and-white segmentation outputs for each image at varying resolutions. Figure~\ref{fig:roughsegmentations} presents the segmentation results for three selected scales: 60\%, 80\%, and 100\%. It is apparent from the visual comparison that the quality of the segmentations varies significantly across different scales.

\begin{figure}[ht]
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{@{}l@{\hspace{5px}}l@{\hspace{5px}}l@{}}
    \includegraphics[width=0.3\linewidth]{figures/test_102_a_60.png} &
    \includegraphics[width=0.3\linewidth]{figures/test_102_a_80.png} \\
    \scriptsize (a) Scale of 60\% & \scriptsize (b) Scale of 80\% \\  
    \includegraphics[width=0.3\linewidth]{figures/test_102_a_100.png} &
    \includegraphics[width=0.3\linewidth]{figures/test_102_a_truth.png} \\
    \scriptsize (c) Scale of 100\% & \scriptsize (d) Groud truth \\
  \end{tabular}
  \caption{Different scales of input image for \textit{CNN} segmentation assessment training}
  \label{fig:roughsegmentations}
\end{figure}

To objectively assess which of the segmentation results is most optimal, we design and train a simple \textit{CNN}-based scoring network. This network consists of a single output neuron that produces a score between 0 and 1, where 0 indicates the poorest segmentation quality and 1 indicates the best (see Fig.~\ref{fig:cnn_network}). The trained \textit{CNN} is employed in the final segmentation stage, as described in Section~\ref{subsec:step6}.

\subsection{CNN training for segmentation results assessment}
\label{subsec:step5}

Using the \textit{UNETSVS} set, we segment each image into nine black-and-white binarizations, producing a total of $300 \times (9 + 1) = 3000$ samples for training the \textit{CNN}. The additional term in the formula accounts for the ground truth image (\textit{GT}), which serves as the expected output from the segmentation algorithm. This ground truth image is also used to compute the Jaccard index \cite{jaccard1901}, which measures the similarity between the segmented output and its corresponding \textit{GT}. The index yields a value between 0 and 1, where 0 indicates a completely incorrect segmentation and 1 indicates a perfect match. The \textit{CNN} architecture used for evaluation consists of a simple stack of convolutional layers, followed by batch normalization and pooling layers. Once the \textit{CNN} scorer is trained, it can be used to evaluate and select the most accurate segmentation result. This final decision-making step is carried out as described in Section~\ref{subsec:step6}.

\subsection{Optimal segmentation step}
\label{subsec:step6}

The final segmentation comprises of three stages.

\subsubsection{Rough stage of final segmentation}
\label{subsubsec:step6_stage1}

The final segmentation algorithm builds upon the approach described in Section~\ref{subsec:step4}. We continue to perform the Rough Segmentation Step (\textit{RSS}), but in this phase, segmentation is carried out at scales ranging from 20\% to 100\%, with a step size of 20\%. From the five resulting segmentation candidates, the one deemed most accurate by the trained \textit{CNN} (introduced in Section~\ref{subsec:step5}) is selected as the final output. The scale at which this optimal segmentation occurs is referred to as the \textit{RSOR}. It is important to note that for smaller images (up to 800$\times$800 pixels), we increase the maximum scale to 150\%. This adjustment accounts for the fact that \textit{U-Net} may underperform when operating on very low-resolution inputs.

\subsubsection{Detailed stage of final segmentation}
\label{subsubsec:step6_stage2}

The \textit{RSOR} ratio plays a crucial role, as it serves as the starting point for the subsequent stage, known as the Detailed Segmentation Step (\textit{DSS}). This step further refines the search for the optimal segmentation scale. Specifically, segmentation is performed at scales ranging from $\textit{RSOR} - 10\%$ to $\textit{RSOR} + 10\%$, using a step size of 4\%. As before, the trained \textit{CNN} from Section~\ref{subsec:step5} is used to evaluate the quality of each segmentation. The scale identified as producing the best result is referred to as the \textit{DSOR}.

\subsubsection{Most detailed stage of final segmentation}
\label{subsubsec:step6_stage3}

With the \textit{DSOR} ratio now established, we proceed to the Most Detailed Segmentation Step (\textit{MDSS}). In this final stage, the input image is rescaled according to the selected \textit{DSOR} value. Unlike in Section~\ref{subsec:step4}, where the window sliding step (\textit{SS}) was set to 64, we now adopt a finer step size of 32 to enhance segmentation precision. The results of this final segmentation step, applied to a sample image from the \textit{DRHAGIS} dataset \cite{holm2017drhagis}, are shown in Figure~\ref{fig:heatmap2}. It is important to note that both the binarized segmentation outputs and the probability heatmaps, originally generated at the scaled resolution, are rescaled back to the original image dimensions for consistency and visualization.

