The common parameters selected for the segmentation process are as follows:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Minimum rough segmentation scale: 20\%,
  \item Maximum rough segmentation scale: 100\%,
  \item Rough segmentation scale step: 20\%,
  \item Rough segmentation range for optimal resolution: $\pm$10\%,
  \item Detailed segmentation range for optimal resolution: $\pm$4\%.
\end{itemize}

The meanings of the column headings used in the evaluation tables are:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textit{RWSS}: Rough Segmentation Window Step,
  \item \textit{DWSS}: Detailed Segmentation Window Step,
  \item \textit{FOWSS}: Final Optimal Window Step for Segmentation,
  \item \textit{TTE}: Total Time Elapsed (in seconds),
  \item \textit{AJI}: Average Jaccard Index,
  \item \textit{MNJI}: Minimum Jaccard Index,
  \item \textit{MXJI}: Maximum Jaccard Index.
\end{itemize}

The highest achieved \textit{AJI} was 0.6153; however, this result came at the cost of a total time elapsed (\textit{TTE}) of 12,877 seconds. In contrast, the fastest configuration, with a \textit{TTE} of just 2,704 seconds, was obtained using the parameters \textit{RWSS} = 128 pixels, \textit{DWSS} = 64 pixels, and \textit{FOWSS} = 32 pixels. This setup yielded an \textit{AJI} of 0.6125, which is only marginally lower than the best result. As such, it represents a significantly more efficient trade-off between segmentation accuracy and computational performance.

\subsection{Second experiment}
\label{subsec:experiment2}

In the second experiment we randomly selected 900 images from \textit{FIVES} dataset. Exactly the same experiments in terms of values for \textbf{RWSS}, \textbf{DWSS} and \textbf{OWSS} were conducted and can be previewed in details in \cite{palles77unetsegmentation}.

The best achieved \textit{AJI} in this experiment was 0.5957, obtained with a total time elapsed (\textit{TTE}) of 8,320 seconds. The fastest configuration, with a \textit{TTE} of just 6,208 seconds, was achieved using the parameters \textit{RWSS} = 128 pixels, \textit{DWSS} = 64 pixels, and \textit{FOWSS} = 32 pixels. However, this setup yielded an \textit{AJI} of only 0.5813, which is noticeably lower than the optimal value. This indicates a trade-off between speed and accuracy, with the faster configuration sacrificing some segmentation performance.

\subsection{Third experiment}
\label{subsec:experiment3}

In the third experiment we decided to try and apply only a part of the Section~\ref{subsubsec:step6_stage3}. We specified the \textit{SS} to values of 8, 16, 32, 64 and 128. You can see the segmentation results in Table~\ref{tab:experiment3}. The results in this experiment are notably inferior to those presented in Section~\ref{subsec:experiment1}. The best outcome observed  corresponds to \textit{SS} of 8, resulting in \textit{TTE} of 24,227 seconds and \textit{AJI} of 0.5639. While this approach demonstrates high execution speed for \textit{SS} values ranging between 32 and 128 pixels, the resulting \textit{AJI} scores remain unsatisfactory. In contrast, the fastest configuration from Section~\ref{subsec:experiment1} achieved an \textit{AJI} of 0.6125, clearly demonstrating the superiority of the hybrid approach described in this article over the traditional \textit{SWA}-based method.

