\section{Conculsions}

The experiments conducted in this article reveal several notable observations regarding the proposed \textit{HUS} algorithm:

\begin{itemize}
  \item When using only 100 training images from the \textit{FIVES} dataset \cite{jin2022fives}, we achieved a higher accuracy of 0.6153 compared to 0.5957 obtained with 900 training images. This suggests that in Section~\ref{subsec:experiment2}, the \textit{U-Net} and \textit{CNN} models may have become overly specialized to the training data, resulting in decreased generalization to the test set.  
  \item In Experiments~\ref{subsec:experiment1} and ~\ref{subsec:experiment2}, we demonstrated that the hybrid method outperforms the classic \textit{SWA} approach by nearly 0.05 in \textit{AJI}, while maintaining comparable execution speeds.  
  \item Our experiments with \textit{HUS} also indicate that high values of the sliding step (\textit{SS}) are not necessarily critical for achieving optimal performance. The results suggest a possible correlation between hierarchical window sizes. Specifically, for a window size of $2^n$, typically associated with the first convolutional layer of \textit{U-Net}, the following configuration yields strong results: $\textit{RWSS} = 2^n$, $\textit{DWSS} = 2^{n-1}$, and $\textit{FOWSS} = 2^{n-2}$.
\end{itemize}

The \textit{HUS} algorithm \cite{palles77unetsegmentation} effectively addresses limitations associated with traditional \textit{U-Net} segmentation:

\begin{itemize}
  \item The requirement for training and test datasets to have similar resolutions.
  \item The substantial memory consumption of \textit{U-Net} when processing high-resolution images.
  \item The sensitivity of classical \textit{U-Net} to variations in the spatial location of the target object.
\end{itemize}

